From the graph:

1. The graph rises as \(x\) moves from the far left until it reaches a local maximum near \(x\approx -2.5\).
2. Then the graph falls until it reaches a local minimum near \(x\approx 1\).
3. After that, it rises again.

Thus, the function is increasing on the intervals where it goes upward and decreasing where it goes downward. Endpoints at the turning points may be included or excluded depending on convention; open intervals are standard.

\[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{Increasing: }&(-\infty,-2.5)\cup(1,\infty),\\[2mm]
\text{Decreasing: }&(-2.5,1).
\end{aligned}
\quad\text{(approximately; using nearest integer estimates, turn at }x\approx -2\text{ and }x\approx 1\text{).}
}
\]
